#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166663 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99f2c276-2eb0-5fe9-b46d-6cf30547a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Z-Score for a Given Probability Using Interval Bisection"}] :question/id #uuid "a2b50673-6a4f-5e64-ae16-fdce0819637e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8526" :question/problem "Approximate the value of $z$ such that $P(Z \\leq z) = 0.1450$ using interval bisection.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1469 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1446 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1423 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1711 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1685 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1660 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1977 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1949 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1922 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the two numbers in the table that $0.1450$ lies between. These numbers are $0.1446$ and $0.1469.$ So, we have the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1446 & <0.1450<0.1469\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above inequality in terms of $\\Phi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.06) & <Φ(z)<Φ(-1.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using interval bisection, we can approximate $z$ as\n\n$$\nz \\approx \\dfrac{-1.06 + (-1.05)}{2} = -1.055.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166664 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a54afd65-a69e-52dd-84eb-7cf936773678" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "d1e64fae-04ef-5c7d-9bb0-e81cf06bb672" :question/math-academy-id "e-8528" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z \\leq z) = 0.60.$\n\n| $\\quad p \\quad$ | $0.60$ | $0.65$ | $0.70$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.2533\\,$ | $\\,0.3853\\,$ | $\\,0.5244\\,$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given information in terms of $\\Phi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le z) & =0.60 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1} (0.60).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p = 0.60,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np = 0.60 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(p) = 0.2533.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz =  \\Phi^{-1}(0.60) = 0.2533.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166673 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8742ad90-1d33-5b56-a83f-8edd7f2f5aef" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table and Symmetry To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "a29f1f2c-104a-53aa-ad55-0c6abde0a359" :question/math-academy-id "e-8590" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z \\leq z) = 0.20.$\n\n| $\\quad p \\quad$ | $0.65$ | $0.70$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.3853\\,$ | $\\,0.5244\\,$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "From the table, notice that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\leq 0.8416 ) = 0.80.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\geq 0.8416 ) = 1 - 0.80 = 0.20.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the standard normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.8416) = 0.20.\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2be6ef2420c8789a0ff94f4f9540533af4134690ebe0c04c50eb02f358dfa266.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.8416) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le -0.8416) & =0.20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required value of $z$ is $-0.8416.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8742ad90-1d33-5b56-a83f-8edd7f2f5aef" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table and Symmetry To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f299df-5afb-5251-a8b9-07729da3e2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3263" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281862} {:answer/id #uuid "31d8df3e-2326-5360-84b5-195a0b825710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2816" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282138} {:answer/id #uuid "61ff013c-5300-5bfb-8245-66276fc6bd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0902" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282524} {:answer/id #uuid "63e35f0e-2deb-5946-85ed-4b07144fa947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.3263" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282549} {:answer/id #uuid "e2198a84-ee66-539d-94ae-5bd4c1e15003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2816" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11f299df-5afb-5251-a8b9-07729da3e2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3263" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281862} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28c3d6d-1eab-598e-9c73-3198876e6a3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85c72120-a302-5186-9506-8f0d0fa22ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164333" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z>z)=0.01.$\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given information in terms of $Φ,$ using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>z) & =0.01 \\\\ 1-P(Z\\le z) & =0.01 \\\\ 1-Φ(z) & =0.01 \\\\ Φ(z) & =1-0.01 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz={Φ}^{-1}(0.99).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.99,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np=0.99\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)=2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)=2.3263.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99f2c276-2eb0-5fe9-b46d-6cf30547a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Z-Score for a Given Probability Using Interval Bisection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7696548-3764-50fe-9203-76d3a45cede8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7696548-3764-50fe-9203-76d3a45cede8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301033} :answer-field/id #uuid "746728e1-7f30-5503-98b8-df427de87a07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f974be1b-50fc-5e85-a97a-26d0236743e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-328306" :question/problem "| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ | $\\,0.9370\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ | $\\,0.9484\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ |\n\nApproximate the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.9480$ using interval bisection and the above table.\n\n$z≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the two numbers in the table that $0.9480$ lies between. These numbers are $0.9474$ and $0.9484.$ So, we have the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.9474 & <0.9480<0.9484\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above inequality in terms of $Φ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.62) & <Φ(z)<Φ(1.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using interval bisection, we can approximate $z$ as\n\n$$\nz≈\\frac{1.62+1.63}{2}=1.625.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a54afd65-a69e-52dd-84eb-7cf936773678" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d57011b-7bdb-5d6e-9649-8309bae4efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0902" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358007} {:answer/id #uuid "acee74f9-0907-5d64-acce-0158fc8e8396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5758" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359245} {:answer/id #uuid "bf99f561-0110-5798-b118-469c2b77aa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0929" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359428} {:answer/id #uuid "d2187c6a-044f-5c3b-b9b8-1b39abd32e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3263" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359588} {:answer/id #uuid "d61fb95f-1972-5160-a45d-84a43d6c34b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2905" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d57011b-7bdb-5d6e-9649-8309bae4efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0902" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358007} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c3987d-8d53-5e98-8e73-e2189f9639df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbb237cf-844c-54a3-a031-530faf349f3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96936" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.999.$\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.900$ | $0.950$ | $0.975$ | $0.990$ | $0.995$ | $0.999$ | $0.9995$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given information in terms of $Φ:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le z) & =0.999 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.999\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz={Φ}^{-1}(0.999).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.999,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np=0.999\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)=3.0902.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz={Φ}^{-1}(0.999)=3.0902.\n$$"}]}
